{
  "book": "GEN",
  "chapter": 43,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-0375e5ad246e4b74": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-87",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Egyptians sat at their meals; reclining was a Persian custom brought in at a later age. See note on Matthew 26:7 (#712). They used chairs of various kinds, and stools, and sometimes sat on the floor with the left leg drawn under them and the right foot planted on the floor, t…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-87",
          "sourceHash": "5f5bd5cc2e8e2adac28162de2f3b4cf29a0bfa9e8423b6bee1646f5d75d544dd",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-1548f22af07f4d4d": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-88",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The ancient Egyptian mode of dining seems to have resembled the Persian rather than the Turkish. Different kinds of food were taken from the large dishes on which the cook had placed them, and were put on one smaller dish which was carried by a servant to the guest. In this insta…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-88",
          "sourceHash": "38ee1109e5ed07196c9ef6b37c48d485cfd9df7e5a881b12d5cec6f31069997e",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-3f6532f21bcebe2f": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-86",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "They set on for him by himself, and for them by themselves, and for the Egyptians, which did eat with him, by themselves: because the Egyptians might not eat bread with the Hebrews; for that is an abomination unto the Egyptians.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-86",
          "sourceHash": "97ef15c093fd4794e11bd0ea510bc5ed672f99b10ec561d2e99173ab1aa547e6",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-869e8eb724bd3a05": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-84",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This is not a benediction, but one of the numerous forms of Oriental salutation used in meeting or in taking leave of all acquaintance.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-84",
          "sourceHash": "72f0675e5224bcf1533f65a581cebd33d1dd3d1ffd5bf11f110563fd43359d1d",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-b9caa54c6e545b94": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-85",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Orientals in general are great eaters of bread. It has been computed that three persons in four live entirely upon it, or else upon such compositions as are made of barley or wheat flour. No doubt the term “bread” was often used to denote food in general; but this was because bre…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-85",
          "sourceHash": "0d11f6ce205561e063b66d16cc788a0e850eeebb9cfbdaa8e82a83c2607c5186",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-f90c9dea6cb53a6e": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-83",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The ancient Egyptians had the beasts they desired for food slaughtered in the courtyard of the dwelling. While the monuments give representations of poulterers’ shops, they do not show any shops for the sale of butchers’ meat, but represent the slaying, in private houses, of quad…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-83",
          "sourceHash": "cee8fc5857af3fa43de4589424774b988375c94f0d8b1a2f9e1066921a3d23bc",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
